# Parsers

## ⛔ BEFORE EDITING ANY GRAMMAR FILE

`docs/architecture/parser/GRAMMAR-REVIEW-STANDARD.md` is the standing brief: its
§2 checklist (items 1-16 at time of writing) applied to **every `const` in the
file** (not sampled), the hard constraints, the oracle verification loop, and
the definition of done (diagnostic clean, lint clean, oracle byte-identical,
macro-buildable clean — not "tests pass"). Use the `grammar-reviewer` agent
before landing; it returns evidence per const, not a verdict.

The naming law is the checklist's *does its name claim a divergence it does not
have?* item: **a dialect prefix (`css…`/`less…`/`scss…`/`jess…`) or an
`Ast`/`Cst` in a rule name is a claim of genuine divergence and must be
earned.** Default to the plain name (`declaration`, `selector`, `atRule`); never
prefix a shared rule; when divergence is real, name the divergence, not the
owner (`declarationWithInterpolatedName`, not `lessDeclaration`). This is a
*duplication* rule with a stated cause — the prefix makes two identical rules
look different, so nobody ever diffs them — and **not** a style guide. The
mechanism and its standing evidence live in the standard's *naming is a
duplication mechanism* section; do not restate them here.

## ⛔ SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR NODE SHAPES

There is no separate builder layer and no legacy production layer: each parser
is a single Parseman grammar module, `src/grammar.ts`, and the `node(...)` /
`field(...)` entries inside it **are** the node-shape specification. The
`src/grammar/{ast,cst}.ts` files are thin per-variant build entries that
re-export one compiled instance each (compiled artifacts are large, so a shared
module would make every consumer ingest all four); they contain no rules.

Dialect layering is expressed by composition, not by copies: Less/SCSS/Jess
compose the CSS base (`packages/syntax/css/css-parser/src/grammar.ts`) via
`composeLeaf` plus the shared recognition material, and override only the
smallest child, value slot, or reference that the dialect actually changes. The
CST and AST variants of one dialect are the *same* rule set built at a different
`hostMode`.

**When you need to know what shape a construct produces:**
1. Read the `node(...)` entry in the owning `src/grammar.ts` — that is the spec.
2. Confirm a change does not move the tree with the byte-identity oracle,
   `packages/syntax/less/less-parser/test/ast-identity-oracle.mjs`. A change that
   moves the tree is a failed change, not a judgement call.

**Do NOT:**
- Guess the shape from observed compiled output or from a test expectation.
- Reverse-engineer a shape by trial-and-error against test expectations.
- Invent "grammar bug" / "leaked CST" rationalizations for a mismatch.

## Packages

- `packages/syntax/css/css-parser`: base CSS parsing
- `packages/syntax/less/less-parser`: LESS parsing
- `packages/syntax/scss/scss-parser`: SCSS parsing (includes sass-spec cache scripts)
- `packages/syntax/jess/jess-parser`: `.jess` parsing
- `packages/parser-shared`: material shared by all four (recognition maps,
  pseudo consts, opaque at-rules) — renamed from `internal-css-recognition` at
  `a74131e8f`. A rule that lives here is by definition shared: the naming law
  applies with full force.

## Typical hotspots

- `src/grammar.ts` (the whole grammar, including node construction)
- `src/cst.ts` / `src/grammar/{ast,cst}.ts` (types and per-variant build entries)
- Tests under `test/**/*.test.ts` (package-dependent)

## Debugging guardrails

- Treat parser and AST objects as potentially circular; avoid `JSON.stringify`
  on them.

## Gating rules

- Prefer `choice`/`dispatch` designs that disambiguate on the first token, so no
  hand-written gate is needed; first-set gating is the mechanism, `not(...)` as
  an end-of-value assertion is the same work done by hand.
- When a gate is unavoidable, keep the gate and the matching alternative shaped
  so they use the smallest practical lookahead.
- Never use unbounded or effectively infinite lookahead for a token choice.
